Generated _B/_U pages land here, and wait here until they are filed.

Filing one means moving it to where it belongs: a band under 000_FRAMEWORK, or a
project under 100_PROJECTS. Drag it in the explorer - folders do not affect the
build. Every .vb under the project is compiled, RootNamespace is empty, and each
file declares its own namespace, so a page compiles wherever it sits.

The two folders to avoid are the ones the project excludes: 900_SANDBOX, tests,
project-backup and restore-points. A page dropped in one of those leaves the
build without saying so.

Regenerating follows the file. PageGenerator looks the page up by name before it
writes, so a filed page is rewritten where it now lives and only a genuinely new
one is written here. Nothing remembers where a page came from.

This file exists because git does not track empty directories, and this folder is
empty whenever nothing is waiting - which is the state it should usually be in.
Without it the folder would vanish from a fresh clone until the next generation
recreated it.
